><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-10.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>This reviewsummarises the methods of cost of sports injury literature&#xD;and highlights the types of expenses associated with sports-related&#xD;injuries.&#xD;• Cost of sports injury studies should consider the use of a cost-of-illness&#xD;(COI) checklist and provide clear statements regarding injury or illness&#xD;identification, the type of costs investigated, the choice of costing&#xD;approach and cost perspective.&#xD;• Further cost of sports injury researchwill benefit various stakeholders&#xD;in the sporting sector, assisting decision-making practices including&#xD;the prioritisation of prevention programmes, targeting the allocation&#xD;of healthcare resources, and informing policy.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="0" rect="595.276001,159.404114,779.276001,251.404114"
/></highlight
